        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          If you process firewood by hand — splitting, stacking, and moving logs without a mechanical processor — a hookaroon is not a luxury. It is the single most important tool in your workflow after the splitting axe or maul itself. The right hookaroon eliminates hundreds of bending-over motions per cord, saves your back, and dramatically speeds up the entire processing chain.
        </p>

        <p>
          The humble hookaroon does not get the glamorous attention that axes, chainsaws, and splitting mauls receive in the firewood world. It is not the tool you show off to visitors. But ask any experienced firewood processor which tool they would least want to lose, and the hookaroon comes up with striking consistency. Once you have incorporated one into your splitting and stacking workflow, going back to bending over and grabbing every piece by hand feels primitive and unnecessarily punishing.
        </p>

        <p>
          The concept is elegantly simple: a curved hook point mounted on a handle, swung into the end grain of a log or split, creating a secure grip that lets you carry, toss, roll, drag, or position the wood without bending your back or wearing out your grip strength. But within that simple concept, there is meaningful variation in hook geometry, handle material, handle length, weight distribution, and overall build quality that separates the tools that genuinely improve your workflow from those that frustrate you with dropped logs, dull points, and poor balance.
        </p>

        <p>
