        </p>

        <h2 id="buying-guide" class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          What to Look for in a Hookaroon
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Hook Geometry</h3>
        <p>
          The shape of the hook is the single most important performance factor. A well-designed hook has a sharp point that penetrates end grain easily, a curved throat that wraps around the wood fibers for a secure grip, and a geometry that allows clean extraction when you release the log. Too sharp and too deep, and the hook buries itself so aggressively that extraction requires a frustrating prying motion. Too shallow, and the hook pulls free under load, dropping the log on your foot. The best hookaroons strike a precise balance — aggressive enough to grip securely, refined enough to release cleanly with a simple wrist flick.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Handle Length</h3>
        <p>
          Handle length determines reach and leverage. Compact models around 19 inches are portable and great for one-handed carry tasks but require more bending when working with logs on the ground. Standard 24 to 28-inch handles provide the best all-around balance of reach and control for most firewood processing tasks. Extended 30-inch-plus handles offer maximum leverage for rolling heavy logs but can feel unwieldy for quick pick-and-toss operations with smaller splits.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Handle Material</h3>
        <p>
          Traditional wood handles — typically hickory or ash — provide excellent shock absorption and a natural, comfortable grip feel. They are also repairable and replaceable. The downside is vulnerability to moisture absorption, cracking in extreme cold or dryness, and eventual breakage under heavy use. Fiberglass and composite handles are virtually indestructible and weather-proof, but they transmit more vibration and lack the warm, organic feel that many woodworkers prefer. Aluminum handles offer the lightest weight but the highest vibration transmission.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Weight and Balance</h3>
        <p>
          A hookaroon should feel balanced and natural in your swing arc. Too much head weight makes the tool front-heavy and fatiguing. Too little head weight requires excessive swing velocity for adequate hook penetration. The sweet spot for most users is a total tool weight between 1.5 and 2.5 pounds, with the balance point falling roughly one-third of the way down from the hook end. Pay attention to how the tool feels in a natural pendulum swing — a well-balanced hookaroon almost swings itself into the target.
        </p>

